 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Boundary Conditions:** Convolution at image edges requires padding (e.g., reflect, replicate) to avoid artifacts [G].
-*   **Zero Sigma:** If $\sigma$ becomes 0, the kernel becomes a Dirac delta (identity). Ensure numerical stability in division by $\sigma$ [G].
-*   **Densification Timing:** Kernel size should be lowered before densifying Gaussians to introduce extra signal on erroneous Gaussians as noise reduces [S4].
-*   **Downscaling:** If images are downsampled, the kernel size must be scaled down by the same factor to maintain equivalent spatial frequency suppression [S4].
+*   **Boundary Effects:** Gaussian convolution at image boundaries can cause artifacts. Padding (e.g., reflection or replication) should be used [G].
+*   **Kernel Size vs. Resolution:** If the kernel size is too large relative to the image resolution, it may oversmooth and remove essential high-frequency details needed for sharp reconstruction [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Downsampling Mismatch:** If downsampling is used, failing to scale the kernel size by $s_{down}$ leads to incorrect frequency suppression [S4].
 
